Chapter: Console

 We say:

Section: Definitions

  effect Console where
    print-line-uni : Text -> [Console.Write] Nothing
    read-line  : [Console.Read] Text

Page 1

Chapter: DeviceMath

 We say:

Section: Clamps and Selection

  real-min : Real, Real -> Real
  real-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  real-max : Real, Real -> Real
  real-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  real-abs : Real -> Real
  real-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

Section: Square Root

  real-sqrt : Real -> Real
  real-sqrt (x) =
    if x <= 0.0 then 0.0
    else dm-sqrt-scaled x 1.0 700

  dm-sqrt-scaled : Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  dm-sqrt-scaled (x) (s) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then s * dm-sqrt-core x
    else if x >= 4.0 then dm-sqrt-scaled (x / 4.0) (s * 2.0) (fuel - 1)
    else if x < 0.25 then dm-sqrt-scaled (x * 4.0) (s * 0.5) (fuel - 1)
    else s * dm-sqrt-core x

  dm-sqrt-core : Real -> Real
  dm-sqrt-core (r) =
    let g0 = (r + 1.0) * 0.5
    in let g1 = (g0 + r / g0) * 0.5
    in let g2 = (g1 + r / g1) * 0.5
    in let g3 = (g2 + r / g2) * 0.5
    in let g4 = (g3 + r / g3) * 0.5
    in (g4 + r / g4) * 0.5

Section: Sine and Cosine

  dm-two-pi : Real
  dm-two-pi = 6.283185307179586

  dm-pi : Real
  dm-pi = 3.141592653589793

  dm-half-pi : Real
  dm-half-pi = 1.570796326794897

  dm-reduce : Real -> Real
  dm-reduce (x) =
    let k = real-from-int (real-to-int (x / dm-two-pi))
    in let r = x - k * dm-two-pi
    in if r > dm-pi then r - dm-two-pi
       else if r < 0.0 - dm-pi then r + dm-two-pi
       else r

  dm-fold-quadrant : Real -> Real
  dm-fold-quadrant (r) =
    if r > dm-half-pi then dm-pi - r
    else if r < 0.0 - dm-half-pi then 0.0 - dm-pi - r
    else r

  dm-sin-poly : Real -> Real
  dm-sin-poly (r) =
    let r2 = r * r
    in let r3 = r2 * r
    in let r5 = r3 * r2
    in let r7 = r5 * r2
    in let r9 = r7 * r2
    in let r11 = r9 * r2
    in r - r3 / 6.0 + r5 / 120.0 - r7 / 5040.0 + r9 / 362880.0 - r11 / 39916800.0

  real-sin : Real -> Real
  real-sin (x) = dm-sin-poly (dm-fold-quadrant (dm-reduce x))

  real-cos : Real -> Real
  real-cos (x) = dm-sin-poly (dm-fold-quadrant (dm-reduce (x + dm-half-pi)))

Chapter: Trig
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath

 Sine, cosine, tangent and arc tangent in Real.

 THIS CHAPTER IS A STAND-IN. Codex already has these in `Gpu chapter DeviceMath`
 and this is the same algorithm -- an argument reduction, a quadrant fold, and an
 11th-order Taylor polynomial -- reproduced only because DeviceMath cannot be
 transpiled to zig at all: its dm-reduce calls real-from-int, and the zig plug has
 no emitter for it. The day that emitter lands, every function here is deleted and
 the citers cite DeviceMath instead. Nothing else in the port should grow a
 dependency on these names.

 The one deliberate difference from DeviceMath: dm-reduce divides by two-pi and
 converts back through the integers, which is exactly the call this arm cannot
 emit, so `wrap` subtracts in a bounded loop instead. That is slower and it is
 exact for the arguments this game has -- yaws, headings and tier angles, all
 within a few turns of zero -- but it is NOT a general reduction, and it must not
 outlive the emitter it works around.

 We say:

Section: Constants

  pi : Real
  pi = 3.141592653589793

  two-pi : Real
  two-pi = 6.283185307179586

  half-pi : Real
  half-pi = 1.570796326794897

  deg : Real
  deg = 0.017453292519943295

Section: Sine and Cosine

  sin-poly : Real -> Real
  sin-poly (r) =
    let r2 = r * r
    in let r3 = r2 * r
    in let r5 = r3 * r2
    in let r7 = r5 * r2
    in let r9 = r7 * r2
    in let r11 = r9 * r2
    in r - r3 / 6.0 + r5 / 120.0 - r7 / 5040.0 + r9 / 362880.0 - r11 / 39916800.0

  fold-quadrant : Real -> Real
  fold-quadrant (r) =
    if r > half-pi then pi - r
    else if r < 0.0 - half-pi then 0.0 - pi - r
    else r

  wrap : Real, Integer -> Real
  wrap (x) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then x
    else if x > pi then wrap (x - two-pi) (fuel - 1)
    else if x < 0.0 - pi then wrap (x + two-pi) (fuel - 1)
    else x

  r-sin : Real -> Real
  r-sin (x) = sin-poly (fold-quadrant (wrap x 64))

  r-cos : Real -> Real
  r-cos (x) = r-sin (x + half-pi)

  r-tan : Real -> Real
  r-tan (x) = r-sin x / r-cos x

Section: Arc Tangent

 THIS ONE IS NOT A STAND-IN. `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` has no atan at all -- its
 whole surface is min, max, abs, sqrt, sin and cos -- and the foreword's other
 arc tangents (Math chapter Cordic, Math chapter Geodesic) are INTEGER milli-unit
 routines, one of which is recorded in PORTING_NOTES A4 as four times less
 accurate than it claims. So a Real atan is a gap in Codex's foreword rather than
 a hole in the zig plug, and it does not go away when the rest of this chapter is
 retired in favour of DeviceMath.

 The method is argument HALVING, not a Taylor series applied directly: the series
 for atan converges hopelessly slowly near |t| = 1, which is exactly where the
 mountains sample it. The half-angle identity

     atan t = 2 * atan (t / (1 + sqrt (1 + t*t)))

 shrinks the argument geometrically, so four applications take |t| <= 1 down to
 about 0.06, where a six-term series is good to roughly 1e-14 and the sixteen it
 is multiplied back by cannot undo that. Above |t| = 1 the reciprocal identity
 folds it back into range first; the screen's outer columns at a pulled-in focal
 do reach |t| near 3, so that branch is live and not defensive.

  atan-half-step : Real -> Real
  atan-half-step (t) = t / (1.0 + real-sqrt (1.0 + t * t))

  atan-halve : Real, Integer -> Real
  atan-halve (t) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then t
    else atan-halve (atan-half-step t) (n - 1)

  atan-series : Real -> Real
  atan-series (u) =
    let u2 = u * u
    in let u3 = u2 * u
    in let u5 = u3 * u2
    in let u7 = u5 * u2
    in let u9 = u7 * u2
    in let u11 = u9 * u2
    in u - u3 / 3.0 + u5 / 5.0 - u7 / 7.0 + u9 / 9.0 - u11 / 11.0

 Four halvings, so the series result is scaled back by 2^4.

  atan-core : Real -> Real
  atan-core (t) = 16.0 * atan-series (atan-halve t 4)

  r-atan : Real -> Real
  r-atan (t) =
    if t > 1.0 then half-pi - atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else if t < 0.0 - 1.0 then (0.0 - half-pi) - atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else atan-core t

Chapter: Geom
  cites Safari chapter Trig

 Pure rider-relative geometry: the coordinate types, the world-to-rider transform,
 the ground curvature, and near-plane clipping. No projection -- that is Camera --
 no drawing, no allocation. The math leaf the rest of the camera stands on.

 A port of wasm/geom.zig.

 We say:

Section: Coordinates

 A ground-plane point measured FROM THE RIDER: how far forward, down his look
 axis, and how far to his right. The fundamental coordinate of the whole scene.

  RiderPt = record {
    right : Real,
    forward : Real
  }

 A rider-relative vertex carrying a height off the ground -- what the road quads
 and raised structures clip and project as.

  Vec3 = record {
    right : Real,
    forward : Real,
    height : Real
  }

 A point in a segment's bottom-left frame: `a` along, `x` across-from-left.

  AX = record {
    a : Real,
    x : Real
  }

Section: Ground

 The local ground is a gentle spherical plateau: a point drops this far below the
 rider's tangent plane at horizontal distance d, so the road bends toward a finite
 horizon instead of a vanishing point at infinity. Larger radius = gentler.

  ground-radius : Real
  ground-radius = 100000.0

  ground-drop : Real, Real -> Real
  ground-drop (right) (forward) = (right * right + forward * forward) / (2.0 * ground-radius)

Section: The Rider Transform

 to-rider maps a point in a segment's bottom-left frame -- `a` along the segment,
 `x` across from its LEFT edge -- into the rider's frame, given his pose along and
 across the segment's centre line and his look angle `yaw`. His pose is
 centre-relative, so the half-width `hw` shifts x to from-the-left.

  to-rider : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  to-rider (a) (x) (cam-along) (cam-across) (yaw) (hw) =
    let d-a = a - cam-along
    in let d-x = x - (cam-across + hw)
    in let c = r-cos yaw
    in let s = r-sin yaw
    in RiderPt { forward = d-a * c + d-x * s, right = (0.0 - d-a) * s + d-x * c }

Section: The Frame Fusion

 next-to-cur maps a point from the NEXT segment's bottom-left frame into the
 CURRENT segment's, across the join. LEFT and RIGHT are NOT mirror images: a left
 turn fuses on the end-left edge, where the origin is the inner corner; a right
 turn fuses on the end-right edge and pays a width shift. `turns-right` is the
 turn direction.

  next-to-cur : Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real -> AX
  next-to-cur (a-b) (x-b) (seg-len) (theta) (turns-right) (width) =
    let c = r-cos theta
    in let s = r-sin theta
    in if turns-right
      then AX { a = a-b * c - x-b * s + width * s + seg-len, x = x-b * c + a-b * s + width * (1.0 - c) }
      else AX { a = a-b * c + x-b * s + seg-len, x = x-b * c - a-b * s }

 cur-to-next is the inverse: a point in the CURRENT segment's frame expressed in
 the NEXT segment's, across the same join. Used to draw the joint just BEHIND the
 rider, so the pavement he is still crossing does not vanish.

  cur-to-next : Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real -> AX
  cur-to-next (a) (x) (seg-len) (theta) (turns-right) (width) =
    let c = r-cos theta
    in let s = r-sin theta
    in if turns-right
      then let a0 = a - seg-len - width * s
        in let x0 = x - width * (1.0 - c)
        in AX { a = a0 * c + x0 * s, x = (0.0 - a0) * s + x0 * c }
      else let a0 = a - seg-len
        in AX { a = a0 * c - x * s, x = a0 * s + x * c }

Section: Line Intersection

 line-meet returns where two infinite lines cross in the rider frame: line A
 through a0 toward a1, line B through b0 toward b1. Used for the corner's outer
 apex, where the two segments' outer shoulders meet. A real turn has a non-zero
 angle, so the lines are never parallel.

  line-meet : RiderPt, RiderPt, RiderPt, RiderPt -> RiderPt
  line-meet (a0) (a1) (b0) (b1) =
    let dax = a1.right - a0.right
    in let daf = a1.forward - a0.forward
    in let dbx = b1.right - b0.right
    in let dbf = b1.forward - b0.forward
    in let t = ((b0.right - a0.right) * dbf - (b0.forward - a0.forward) * dbx) / (dax * dbf - daf * dbx)
    in RiderPt { right = a0.right + t * dax, forward = a0.forward + t * daf }

Section: Near-Plane Clipping

 Clip a rider-frame polygon against the near plane at `near` metres forward, so no
 vertex sits behind the eye where the perspective divide would fling it across the
 screen. Fewer than 3 vertices out means the caller skips the polygon.

 The zig writes into a caller-supplied `out` slice and returns a count; this
 returns the list, which is the same thing without the buffer. The wrap-around
 edge is the reason the index walks to the length and reads (i + 1) modulo it: the
 polygon is closed, so the last vertex pairs with the first.

  clip-cross : Vec3, Vec3, Real -> List Vec3
  clip-cross (a) (b) (near) =
    let f = (near - a.forward) / (b.forward - a.forward)
    in [Vec3 { right = a.right + f * (b.right - a.right), forward = near, height = a.height + f * (b.height - a.height) }]

  both-sides : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  both-sides (a-in) (b-in) = if a-in then b-in else (if b-in then False else True)

  clip-near-edge : List Vec3, Real, Integer -> List Vec3
  clip-near-edge (poly) (near) (i) =
    let n = list-length poly
    in if i >= n then []
    else let a = list-at poly i
    in let b = list-at poly ((i + 1) - (((i + 1) / n) * n))
    in let a-in = a.forward >= near
    in let b-in = b.forward >= near
    in let kept = if a-in then [a] else []
    in let crossed = if both-sides a-in b-in then [] else clip-cross a b near
    in kept & crossed & clip-near-edge poly near (i + 1)

  clip-near : List Vec3, Real -> List Vec3
  clip-near (poly) (near) = clip-near-edge poly near 0

Chapter: Camera
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom

 The perspective projection: a rider-relative ground point at a height becomes a
 screen pixel. Owns the lens constants and the near plane. Pure; no drawing, no
 allocation.

 A port of wasm/camera.zig.

 ONE ADAPTATION. The zig carries the live projection width as a mutable global,
 `pub var view_w`, which the native window widens toward the screen's aspect while
 FOCAL stays frozen -- that is what makes a wider window a wider field of view
 rather than a zoom. Codex has no mutable global, so view-w is threaded as a
 parameter. It is the camera's only mutable state, and threading it is the pure
 equivalent rather than a change of behaviour: pass camera-w for the design width
 the browser and the golden-PNG harness both use.

 We say:

Section: Lens

  camera-w : Real
  camera-w = 960.0

  camera-h : Real
  camera-h = 600.0

  eye-h : Real
  eye-h = 1.2

  near : Real
  near = 0.4

  fov-deg : Real
  fov-deg = 70.0

 The base focal, in pixels, looking straight ahead. FROZEN at the design width and
 NOT recomputed from the live view-w -- that is what keeps a wider window a wider
 FOV. The live cam-focal pulls IN from this as the rider leans or narrows focus.

  focal : Real
  focal = (camera-w / 2.0) / r-tan (fov-deg / 2.0 * deg)

Section: Focal Pull-In

 The focal pulls IN -- widening the FOV, a smaller focal -- for the deeper of two
 narrowings: the LEAN into a turn, and the rider's ATTENTION on something off the
 road. The lean narrows hardest and is squared so it snaps in deep in the turn;
 attention narrows gentler and linearly, but a crossing cat's attention overshoots
 1, so it can pull harder than a full gaze. They rarely overlap -- eyes on the road
 mid-corner -- so the tightest simply wins.

  min-focal-factor : Real
  min-focal-factor = 0.35

  min-gaze-focal-factor : Real
  min-gaze-focal-factor = 0.61

  focal-for-lean : Real -> Real
  focal-for-lean (lean-frac) = focal * (1.0 - (1.0 - min-focal-factor) * lean-frac * lean-frac)

  focal-for-gaze : Real -> Real
  focal-for-gaze (attention) = focal * (1.0 - (1.0 - min-gaze-focal-factor) * attention)

  cam-focal : Real, Real -> Real
  cam-focal (lean-frac) (attention) =
    let a = focal-for-lean lean-frac
    in let b = focal-for-gaze attention
    in if a < b then a else b

Section: Projection

  ScreenPt = record {
    x : Real,
    y : Real
  }

 The perspective divide is by forward, so callers must clip to the near plane
 first -- forward is then never near zero.

  project : Vec3, Real, Real -> ScreenPt
  project (p) (cf) (view-w) =
    ScreenPt {
      x = view-w / 2.0 + (p.right / p.forward) * cf,
      y = camera-h / 2.0 - ((p.height - eye-h) / p.forward) * cf
    }

Chapter: Paint
  cites Safari chapter Camera

 The seam to JS: a draw command in wasm/paint.zig's wire.

 The zig writes each command into a fixed pre-sized buffer in linear memory and
 hands the browser a byte length. The port is pure, so it RETURNS commands and the
 wasm shim does the writing -- the same split NOTES calls seam 3, and the reason
 one grader serves every module: a frame is a flat list either way.

 The wire, per polygon:

   tag 0   [0][color][nPoints][x f32][y f32]...
   tag 1   [1][color][strength f32][nPoints][x f32][y f32]...

 tag 0 is a solid fill; tag 1 is a horizontal round gradient -- dark edge, bright
 centre, dark edge across the polygon's x-extent -- with a per-polygon strength
 that lets crown shading fade to flat with distance instead of popping. strength
 rides on every command so the record has one shape; tag 0 ignores it, exactly as
 the blitter does.

 Coordinates are SCREEN pixels here and still Real. They narrow to f32 in the
 shim, at the buffer, which is the only place the game's width is load-bearing.

 We say:

Section: Commands

  DrawCmd = record {
    tag : Integer,
    color : Integer,
    strength : Real,
    pts : List Real
  }

  flatten-screen : List ScreenPt, Integer -> List Real
  flatten-screen (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else let p = list-at ps i
    in [p.x, p.y] & flatten-screen ps (i + 1)

  push-poly : Integer, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-poly (color) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 0, color = color, strength = 0.0, pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

 pushRoundPoly drops a polygon with fewer than three points rather than emitting
 a degenerate one; push-poly is given the same guard so the two agree.

  push-round-poly : Integer, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-round-poly (color) (strength) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 1, color = color, strength = strength, pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

Chapter: Tree
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 A roadside conifer, with two independent detail axes the renderer sets per tree
 because they are noticed at different ranges: the TRUNK is a round-shaded cylinder
 up close and a flat bar beyond, and the CROWN is eight 3D cone tiers near and
 eight flat triangles far. A cone is a base circle of world points, each at its own
 depth, plus the apex, projected and filled to its convex-hull silhouette, so the
 near rim is genuinely nearer and foreshortens.

 The crown is SOLID either way -- its roundness is the SHAPE, not lighting -- which
 keeps it uniform across the tree line with no shaded-to-solid pop, since crowns
 are seen far out.

 A port of wasm/tree.zig.

 The hull is PORTED, not cited, though Math chapter Geometry has the same
 algorithm. Two reasons, and the second is the binding one. Geometry's diverges for
 fewer than three points -- it returns them sorted where the zig returns them as
 given. And chapters share ONE FLAT NAMESPACE inside a bundle: Geometry defines its
 own Vec3, which collides with Geom's, and the unit will not compile. Citing a
 foreword chapter is not free of a port's own type names.

 We say:

Section: Proportions

 The eight crown tiers as fractions of the foliage span: apex and base height, and
 base half-width. Narrow at the top, widest at the bottom.

  tier-top : List Real
  tier-top = [0.0, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.40, 0.50, 0.60, 0.70]

  tier-bot : List Real
  tier-bot = [0.30, 0.40, 0.50, 0.60, 0.70, 0.80, 0.90, 1.0]

  tier-wide : List Real
  tier-wide = [0.35, 0.44, 0.53, 0.63, 0.72, 0.81, 0.91, 1.0]

  trunk-color : Integer
  trunk-color = 5914146

  visible-trunk : Real
  visible-trunk = 0.44

  crown-h : Real
  crown-h = 0.648

  crown-w : Real
  crown-w = 0.288

 A cone base nearer than this bails to the flat tier.

  min-cone-forward : Real
  min-cone-forward = 0.4

  ring-n : Integer
  ring-n = 16

Section: Metrics

 The screen-space layout shared by both forms: ground point, pixel height, crown
 apex, foliage span, and crown base half-width.

  Metrics = record {
    bx : Real,
    by : Real,
    ht : Real,
    apex-y : Real,
    foliage : Real,
    w : Real
  }

  metrics : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Metrics
  metrics (right) (forward) (height) (cf) (view-w) =
    let base = project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let top = project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = height }) cf view-w
    in let ht = base.y - top.y
    in let foliage = ht * crown-h
    in let crown-bottom-y = base.y - ht * visible-trunk
    in Metrics { bx = base.x, by = base.y, ht = ht, apex-y = crown-bottom-y - foliage, foliage = foliage, w = ht * crown-w }

Section: The Trunk

  draw-trunk : Metrics, Boolean -> List DrawCmd
  draw-trunk (m) (round-trunk) =
    let trunk-w = if m.ht * 0.08 > 1.0 then m.ht * 0.08 else 1.0
    in let trunk-h = m.ht * visible-trunk + m.ht * 0.05
    in let tx = m.bx - trunk-w / 2.0
    in let pts = [ScreenPt { x = tx, y = m.by - trunk-h }, ScreenPt { x = tx + trunk-w, y = m.by - trunk-h }, ScreenPt { x = tx + trunk-w, y = m.by }, ScreenPt { x = tx, y = m.by }]
    in if round-trunk then push-round-poly trunk-color 1.0 pts else push-poly trunk-color pts

Section: The Flat Crown

 One flat crown tier: the far and cheap form, and the near form's bail-out for a
 cone too close to project.

  tier-triangle : Metrics, Integer, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  tier-triangle (m) (k) (color) =
    let tri = [ScreenPt { x = m.bx, y = m.apex-y + m.foliage * list-at tier-top k }, ScreenPt { x = m.bx + m.w * list-at tier-wide k, y = m.apex-y + m.foliage * list-at tier-bot k }, ScreenPt { x = m.bx - m.w * list-at tier-wide k, y = m.apex-y + m.foliage * list-at tier-bot k }]
    in push-poly color tri

Section: The Cone Crown

 Crown tier k as a cone: a base circle of world radius R on the trunk axis rising
 to the tier apex. Project the apex and a ring of base points, each at its own
 depth, and fill their convex hull -- exactly the cone's silhouette, a cone being
 convex.

 The ring angle is carried as a Real accumulator rather than derived from the
 index, because the zig plug has no emitter for real-from-int.

  cone-ring : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real -> List ScreenPt
  cone-ring (r0) (f0) (rad) (h-base) (cf) (view-w) (i) (a) =
    if i >= ring-n then []
    else let p = Vec3 { right = r0 + rad * r-cos a, forward = f0 + rad * r-sin a, height = h-base }
    in [project p cf view-w] & cone-ring r0 f0 rad h-base cf view-w (i + 1) (a + two-pi / 16.0)

 The tie test is ~0, BITWISE exact, not ~. Codex refuses == on a Real (CDX2085)
 and makes the caller say which equality it means; the zig writes a plain ==,
 which is the bitwise one. A 4-ULP ~ here would call distinct ring points equal
 and reorder the sort, changing the hull's point count.

  less-xy : ScreenPt, ScreenPt -> Boolean
  less-xy (a) (b) = if a.x < b.x then True else (if a.x ~0 b.x then a.y < b.y else False)

  hull-insert : ScreenPt, List ScreenPt, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  hull-insert (p) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then xs & [p]
    else if less-xy p (list-at xs i) then (list-take xs i) & [p] & (list-drop xs i)
    else hull-insert p xs (i + 1)

  sort-pts : List ScreenPt, Integer, List ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  sort-pts (src) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length src then acc
    else sort-pts src (i + 1) (hull-insert (list-at src i) acc 0)

 Trim while the last two hull points and the candidate turn the wrong way. The
 comparison is <= 0, so a collinear point is dropped rather than kept -- that is
 the zig's choice and it changes the point COUNT, which the judge compares exactly.

  hull-trim : List ScreenPt, ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  hull-trim (hull) (p) =
    let n = list-length hull
    in if n < 2 then hull
    else let a = list-at hull (n - 2)
    in let b = list-at hull (n - 1)
    in let cr = (b.x - a.x) * (p.y - a.y) - (b.y - a.y) * (p.x - a.x)
    in if cr <= 0.0 then hull-trim (list-take hull (n - 1)) p else hull

  lower-chain : List ScreenPt, Integer, List ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  lower-chain (ps) (i) (hull) =
    if i >= list-length ps then hull
    else let p = list-at ps i
    in lower-chain ps (i + 1) ((hull-trim hull p) & [p])

  upper-chain : List ScreenPt, Integer, List ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  upper-chain (ps) (i) (hull) =
    if i < 0 then hull
    else let p = list-at ps i
    in upper-chain ps (i - 1) ((hull-trim hull p) & [p])

 Andrew's monotone chain: sort, build the lower and upper chains, and concatenate
 dropping the last of each because it is the first of the other.

  convex-hull-pts : List ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  convex-hull-pts (ps) =
    let n = list-length ps
    in if n < 3 then ps
    else let sorted = sort-pts ps 0 []
    in let lo = lower-chain sorted 0 []
    in let up = upper-chain sorted (n - 1) []
    in (list-take lo (list-length lo - 1)) & (list-take up (list-length up - 1))

  tier-cone : Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real, Real, Metrics, Integer, Real -> List DrawCmd
  tier-cone (r0) (f0) (height) (color) (cf) (view-w) (m) (k) (shade) =
    let rad = crown-w * list-at tier-wide k * height
    in if f0 - rad < min-cone-forward then tier-triangle m k color
    else let h-base = (visible-trunk + crown-h * (1.0 - list-at tier-bot k)) * height
    in let h-apex = (visible-trunk + crown-h * (1.0 - list-at tier-top k)) * height
    in let apex = project (Vec3 { right = r0, forward = f0, height = h-apex }) cf view-w
    in let ring = (cone-ring r0 f0 rad h-base cf view-w 0 0.0) & [apex]
    in let hull = convex-hull-pts ring
    in if list-length hull < 3 then []
    else push-round-poly color shade hull

Section: The Tree

  tiers : Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real, Real, Metrics, Boolean, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  tiers (r0) (f0) (height) (color) (cf) (view-w) (m) (near-crown) (shade) (k) =
    if k >= 8 then []
    else let one = if near-crown then tier-cone r0 f0 height color cf view-w m k shade else tier-triangle m k color
    in one & tiers r0 f0 height color cf view-w m near-crown shade (k + 1)

 A tree less than a pixel tall is not drawn at all.

  tree-draw : Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real, Real, Boolean, Boolean, Real -> List DrawCmd
  tree-draw (right) (forward) (height) (color) (cf) (view-w) (round-trunk) (near-crown) (shade) =
    let m = metrics right forward height cf view-w
    in if m.ht < 1.0 then []
    else (draw-trunk m round-trunk) & tiers right forward height color cf view-w m near-crown shade 0

Chapter: Pond

 pond -- a small pond at a corner (seg13), just BEYOND a right-turn intersection,
 off to its LEFT, with ducks floating on the water. Pure data and dimensions; the
 mapping, projection and emit live in the renderer, the same split safari-critter
 uses.

 Frame: cv = metres BEYOND the incoming segment's end edge (the rider's forward as
 he arrives), cu = metres across from its end-left corner (0 = left edge, + = toward
 the road; the pond's cu is NEGATIVE, out to the left -- the outer side of the right
 turn).

 A port of wasm/pond.zig. The zig carries these as f32 and this chapter as Real
 (f64), so the judge compares with a tolerance rather than for equality; every
 value here is integral except duck-height, so the tolerance buys nothing yet and
 the comparison is effectively exact.

 We say:

Section: Shapes

  PondPt = record {
    cu : Real,
    cv : Real
  }

  Duck = record {
    p : PondPt,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

Section: Water

 The outline is a big blob off the LEFT of the road, ~30m across (cu -1 to -31)
 reaching ~30m beyond the intersection (cv 3 to 32). Its near edge (the flat front
 at cv 3) faces the incoming road.

  water-outline : List PondPt
  water-outline =
    [ PondPt { cu = -2.0, cv = 3.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -28.0, cv = 3.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -31.0, cv = 14.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 28.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 32.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -1.0, cv = 16.0 } ]

  water-color : Integer
  water-color = 3112588

Section: Bank

 A 1m khaki bank along the water's FAR edge -- the shore. Its inner edge hugs the
 water's far edge (the arc cv 29 to 32 to 28), its outer edge is 1m further onto
 the land.

  bank : List PondPt
  bank =
    [ PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 32.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 28.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 33.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 30.0 } ]

  bank-color : Integer
  bank-color = 12759680

Section: Ducks

 Six ducks floating on the water, spread across the pond -- three deeper in (larger
 cv) and three nearer the intersection (smaller cv, toward the front edge at cv 3).
 ADULTS, drawn slightly out of proportion so they read. cu is shifted toward the
 road (less negative) than the pond centre, so the ducks hug the near/road side of
 the water while still sitting inside the outline.

  duck-codepoint : Integer
  duck-codepoint = 129414

  duck-height : Real
  duck-height = 0.9

  ducks : List Duck
  ducks =
    [ Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -8.0, cv = 11.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -16.0, cv = 17.0 }, face-right = False },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -9.0, cv = 21.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -4.0, cv = 6.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -12.0, cv = 7.0 }, face-right = False },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -20.0, cv = 8.0 }, face-right = True } ]

Chapter: GuardRail
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The metal barrier on the outer edge of a corner: one horizontal bar on thin
 upright posts. Render builds the PATH the rail follows along the ground -- run-up
 along the outer shoulder, the two legs into and out of the apex, run-out -- and
 this chapter raises that path into 3D rail polygons.

 A port of wasm/guard_rail.zig.

 WHY IT COLLECTS INSTEAD OF DRAWING. Rails, trees and critters share ONE
 back-to-front depth pass, so a tree standing behind a rail is occluded by it.
 Each rail poly therefore carries its own mean forward depth and is merged into
 render's sort; the clip-project-push happens later, in sorted order. Drawing
 rails in a separate pass under all scenery is the exact bug that once made a far
 tree paint over a near rail. So rail-emit and rail-draw-poly are deliberately two
 functions, and the ORDERING BETWEEN THEM BELONGS TO RENDER.

 The bar is a quad STRIP -- one quad per path segment -- not the single long
 ribbon the TypeScript draws. Perceptually identical, since consecutive quads
 share an exact edge, but every poly stays at four vertices, so it near-clips
 cleanly and sorts by an honest per-poly depth. Heights are raw, with no ground
 curvature, exactly as in the TypeScript: the rail is always close and the drop is
 sub-millimetre.

 THIS IS THE FIRST CHAPTER TO CITE `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` RATHER THAN THE
 PORT'S OWN STAND-IN. real-sqrt does not call dm-reduce, so it never reaches the
 real-from-int the zig plug cannot emit, and the emitter emits only what the entry
 reaches. `Safari chapter Trig` is still needed for sine and cosine and is still a
 stand-in; the square root is not one. Nothing here should acquire a Trig name.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

 The bar's centre above the ground, its vertical thickness, and each upright
 post's width -- all in metres.

  rail-height : Real
  rail-height = 0.5

  rail-thickness : Real
  rail-thickness = 0.1

  rail-post-width : Real
  rail-post-width = 0.02

 The bar reads bright metallic; the posts sit a touch darker behind it.

  rail-metal : Integer
  rail-metal = 12765135

  rail-post-metal : Integer
  rail-post-metal = 10133672

 How far the rail runs past the corner along each outer edge. Render's to use, but
 it is the rail's dimension and the zig publishes it here.

  rail-runout : Integer
  rail-runout = 10

 The zig's store is a fixed array whose add silently returns once full. The cap is
 sized so that cannot fire on this route, but a silent drop is still the stated
 behaviour, so the port keeps it -- as a list-take over the finished list, which is
 exactly the same thing: bars are added before posts in both, so truncating the
 concatenation drops precisely the elements the zig would never have stored.

  max-rail-polys : Integer
  max-rail-polys = 3072

  bar-top : Real
  bar-top = rail-height + rail-thickness / 2.0

  bar-bot : Real
  bar-bot = rail-height - rail-thickness / 2.0

  half-post : Real
  half-post = rail-post-width / 2.0

Section: A Raised Poly

 One raised rail poly in the rider frame -- always four vertices -- carrying its
 fill colour and its mean forward depth, the key render sorts it by.

  RailPoly = record {
    v : List Vec3,
    color : Integer,
    fwd : Real
  }

  rail-poly : Vec3, Vec3, Vec3, Vec3, Integer -> RailPoly
  rail-poly (p0) (p1) (p2) (p3) (color) =
    let fwd = (p0.forward + p1.forward + p2.forward + p3.forward) / 4.0
    in RailPoly { v = [p0, p1, p2, p3], color = color, fwd = fwd }

Section: The Bar

 A quad strip between consecutive path points, bottom edge at bar-bot and top edge
 at bar-top. Consecutive quads share an edge exactly, so it reads as one band.

  bar-quad : RiderPt, RiderPt -> RailPoly
  bar-quad (p) (q) =
    let p-bot = Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = bar-bot }
    in let q-bot = Vec3 { right = q.right, forward = q.forward, height = bar-bot }
    in let q-top = Vec3 { right = q.right, forward = q.forward, height = bar-top }
    in let p-top = Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = bar-top }
    in rail-poly p-bot q-bot q-top p-top rail-metal

  bars : List RiderPt, Integer -> List RailPoly
  bars (path) (i) =
    if i + 1 >= list-length path then []
    else [bar-quad (list-at path i) (list-at path (i + 1))] & bars path (i + 1)

Section: The Posts

 A slim upright at each path point, its width laid ALONG the local run direction so
 it reads edge-on from the road. The direction is taken from the neighbours on
 either side, clamped at both ends of the path.

 THE ZERO TEST IS ~0, BITWISE EXACT, NOT ~. The zig writes `if (len == 0)`, which
 is a bitwise comparison, and Codex refuses a bare == between Reals (CDX2085), so
 the caller has to say which equality it meant. A 4-ULP ~ would call a
 nearly-coincident pair of path points coincident and swing that post to the fixed
 fallback direction instead of its own -- a visible wrong answer, not a rounding
 one. Same call as the hull sort in Tree, for the same reason.

  post-box : RiderPt, Real, Real -> RailPoly
  post-box (p) (ox) (ofwd) =
    let back-foot = Vec3 { right = p.right - ox, forward = p.forward - ofwd, height = 0.0 }
    in let fore-foot = Vec3 { right = p.right + ox, forward = p.forward + ofwd, height = 0.0 }
    in let fore-head = Vec3 { right = p.right + ox, forward = p.forward + ofwd, height = bar-top }
    in let back-head = Vec3 { right = p.right - ox, forward = p.forward - ofwd, height = bar-top }
    in rail-poly back-foot fore-foot fore-head back-head rail-post-metal

  post-quad : List RiderPt, Integer -> RailPoly
  post-quad (path) (i) =
    let n = list-length path
    in let ia = if i == 0 then 0 else i - 1
    in let ib = if i + 1 >= n then n - 1 else i + 1
    in let a = list-at path ia
    in let b = list-at path ib
    in let dr = b.right - a.right
    in let df = b.forward - a.forward
    in let raw = real-sqrt (dr * dr + df * df)
    in let run = if raw ~0 0.0 then 1.0 else raw
    in let ox = dr / run * half-post
    in let ofwd = df / run * half-post
    in post-box (list-at path i) ox ofwd

  posts : List RiderPt, Integer -> List RailPoly
  posts (path) (i) =
    if i >= list-length path then []
    else [post-quad path i] & posts path (i + 1)

Section: Emit

 Raise the ground path -- its centreline in the rider frame, one post per point --
 into the bar and its posts. A path of fewer than two points has no run direction
 and raises nothing.

  rail-emit : List RiderPt -> List RailPoly
  rail-emit (path) =
    if list-length path < 2 then []
    else list-take ((bars path 0) & posts path 0) max-rail-polys

Section: Draw

 Near-clip one stored rail poly, project it, and push the fill -- the same pipeline
 as a road quad, but the corners carry their own heights so it stands above the
 pavement. Called by render in depth-sorted order.

  project-all : List Vec3, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  project-all (ps) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [project (list-at ps i) cf view-w] & project-all ps cf view-w (i + 1)

  rail-draw-poly : RailPoly, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rail-draw-poly (rp) (cf) (view-w) =
    let clipped = clip-near rp.v near
    in if list-length clipped < 3 then []
    else push-poly rp.color (project-all clipped cf view-w 0)

 A convenience for a caller that already holds the polys in the order it wants them
 painted. THE ORDER IS THE CALLER'S: render merges rails into one depth sort with
 the trees and the critters, and this walks the list exactly as given.

  rail-draw-all : List RailPoly, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  rail-draw-all (ps) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else (rail-draw-poly (list-at ps i) cf view-w) & rail-draw-all ps cf view-w (i + 1)

Chapter: Sky
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Camera

 The day-to-dusk clock: the sky's colour, and the setting sun's screen placement.
 Pure functions of the STEP clock -- safari.zig owns it -- and the rider's absolute
 heading.

 A port of wasm/sky.zig.

 THE ZIG OWNS EVERY COLOUR AND POSITION. The blitter paints the gradients it is
 handed and does no clock or colour arithmetic, so this chapter is the whole of
 the sunset. The sun draws BEHIND the mountains, because the blitter paints it
 before the buffer whose first polygons are the ranges.

 THIS IS THE FIRST CHAPTER THAT NEEDS real-to-int, and it needs it twice: to
 round a lerped colour channel and to pack three of them into one integer. Until
 2026-08-29 the zig plug could emit neither conversion and this module was simply
 unportable -- see PORTING_NOTES A1, and plugs-backlog row 2.03 for the emitter
 that closed it. Nothing here works around anything any more.

 We say:

Section: The Sun as a Clock

 Its height drops linearly with the step, so it scrubs on reverse and freezes on
 pause like everything else. Heights are PIXELS at the base focal, so they compare
 directly with the mountain silhouettes.

  sun-bearing : Real
  sun-bearing = 0.0 - 2.2176

  sun-radius-px : Real
  sun-radius-px = 46.0

 Step-0 height, calibrated to the route's timing in the TypeScript.

  sun-start-px : Real
  sun-start-px = 244.0

  sun-drop-px-per-step : Real
  sun-drop-px-per-step = 0.41616 * (2.0 * sun-radius-px) / 625.0

 The whole disc is below the horizon at this height.

  sun-fully-set-px : Real
  sun-fully-set-px = 0.0 - sun-radius-px

 How far from the horizon the sunset red fades out, and how far off-heading the
 sun may be before it is off-screen entirely.

  warmth-falloff-px : Real
  warmth-falloff-px = 110.0

  visible-bearing-limit : Real
  visible-bearing-limit = 1.4

  sun-height-px : Real -> Real
  sun-height-px (step) = sun-start-px - sun-drop-px-per-step * step

Section: Dusk

 How far dusk has progressed, 0 = day and 1 = night. While the sun is up the
 darkening is a SQUARED ramp -- barely moving while it is high, accelerating
 toward sunset -- so the sky stays bright until around sunset; once the disc is
 fully set the rest fades linearly.

  dusk-at-set : Real
  dusk-at-set = 0.5 * (sun-start-px - sun-fully-set-px) / sun-start-px

 The two arms are separate definitions rather than one expression, because Codex
 refuses a `let` nested inside an `if` arm inside a `let` (CDX1023). Flattening is
 the fix and it reads better here anyway.

  dusk-while-up : Real -> Real
  dusk-while-up (h) =
    let p = (sun-start-px - h) / (sun-start-px - sun-fully-set-px)
    in real-max 0.0 (dusk-at-set * p * p)

  dusk-after-set : Real -> Real
  dusk-after-set (h) =
    real-min 1.0 (dusk-at-set + (sun-fully-set-px - h) / sun-radius-px * (1.0 - dusk-at-set))

  sun-set-fraction : Real -> Real
  sun-set-fraction (step) =
    let h = sun-height-px step
    in if h >= sun-fully-set-px then dusk-while-up h else dusk-after-set h

 How RED the afterglow is, 0 to 1: it peaks as the disc crosses the horizon and
 fades when the sun is high or deep below. This drives the warm band the sky mixes
 into its lower edge.

  sunset-warmth : Real -> Real
  sunset-warmth (step) =
    real-max 0.0 (1.0 - real-abs (sun-height-px step) / warmth-falloff-px)

Section: Colours

 The upper sky dims ALL channels toward dusk, so it deepens rather than greying
 out; the red mixes only into the lower horizon band.

  Rgb = record {
    r : Real,
    g : Real,
    b : Real
  }

  day-sky : Rgb
  day-sky = Rgb { r = 142.0, g = 202.0, b = 230.0 }

  dusk-sky : Rgb
  dusk-sky = Rgb { r = 36.0, g = 58.0, b = 94.0 }

  sunset-red : Rgb
  sunset-red = Rgb { r = 222.0, g = 88.0, b = 52.0 }

 How strongly the red mixes in at peak warmth.

  sunset-glow : Real
  sunset-glow = 0.85

 Zig's @round goes to nearest and ties AWAY FROM ZERO, which is not what
 truncation gives, so the half has to be added before the conversion and the sign
 handled. Every channel this rounds is in 0..255 and the negative arm is
 unreachable from here -- it is written because a round that is wrong for half its
 domain is a trap for the next caller, and mountains.zig is already the next
 caller.

  round-real : Real -> Real
  round-real (x) =
    if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - real-from-int (real-to-int (0.5 - x))
    else real-from-int (real-to-int (x + 0.5))

  lerp3 : Rgb, Rgb, Real -> Rgb
  lerp3 (a) (b) (t) =
    Rgb {
      r = round-real (a.r + (b.r - a.r) * t),
      g = round-real (a.g + (b.g - a.g) * t),
      b = round-real (a.b + (b.b - a.b) * t)
    }

 The wire is one 0xRRGGBB integer, shifted and or-ed exactly as the zig does.
 Arithmetic would give the same answer for channels this size, but the shift is
 what the source says and the plug emits it.

  pack : Rgb -> Integer
  pack (c) =
    bit-or (bit-or (bit-shl (real-to-int c.r) 16) (bit-shl (real-to-int c.g) 8)) (real-to-int c.b)

  sky-color : Real -> Integer
  sky-color (step) = pack (lerp3 day-sky dusk-sky (sun-set-fraction step))

  horizon-color : Real -> Integer
  horizon-color (step) =
    let sky = lerp3 day-sky dusk-sky (sun-set-fraction step)
    in pack (lerp3 sky sunset-red (sunset-warmth step * sunset-glow))

Section: The Sun's Placement

 `scale` is cam-focal over the base focal: it squeezes the disc and its glow
 vertically on a lean, and is 1.0 in the static frame. The blitter multiplies its
 radii by it. `visible` is False when the sun is too far off-heading to be on
 screen, and the blitter then skips it entirely.

  SunPos = record {
    visible : Boolean,
    x : Real,
    y : Real,
    scale : Real
  }

  sun-pos-visible : Real, Real, Real, Real -> SunPos
  sun-pos-visible (rel) (step) (cam-focal) (view-w) =
    let v-scale = cam-focal / focal
    in SunPos { visible = True, x = view-w / 2.0 + r-tan rel * cam-focal, y = camera-h / 2.0 - sun-height-px step * v-scale, scale = v-scale }

 The x centre follows the LIVE width, which is the peripheral-FOV rule the camera
 keeps everywhere: a wider window sees more, it does not zoom.

  sun-pos : Real, Real, Real, Real -> SunPos
  sun-pos (heading) (step) (cam-focal) (view-w) =
    let rel = wrap (sun-bearing - heading) 64
    in if real-abs rel >= visible-bearing-limit
      then SunPos { visible = False, x = 0.0, y = 0.0, scale = 0.0 }
      else sun-pos-visible rel step cam-focal view-w

Chapter: Mountains
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The far backdrop ranges, as screen-space silhouettes. Each range is a pure
 function from absolute bearing -- radians, 0 = north, + = clockwise -- to a height
 in pixels above the horizon; the frame samples it per column across the screen and
 fills the silhouette. Drawn FIRST, behind the road.

 A port of wasm/mountains.zig.

 The light DIMS the rock and the snow toward dusk, handed down as a `dusk`
 fraction from the sunset clock. The rolling LAND stays put, matching the grass.

 THE COLOUR HELPERS ARE SKY'S, NOT COPIES. snowColor is a lerp between two
 colours followed by a round and a pack, which is exactly `lerp3` and `pack` in
 Sky, and mountains.zig imports sky.zig for the sun as well. Reusing them keeps
 one rounding rule in the port instead of two that can drift.

 We say:

Section: Palette

  rock : Integer
  rock = 5991055

  rock-west : Integer
  rock-west = 3752799

  land : Integer
  land = 4886339

 Rock fades to this fraction of its day brightness by night -- it stays BELOW the
 snow, which is also dimming.

  rock-night-dim : Real
  rock-night-dim = 0.5

  snow-day : Rgb
  snow-day = Rgb { r = 238.0, g = 243.0, b = 248.0 }

  snow-night : Rgb
  snow-night = Rgb { r = 70.0, g = 84.0, b = 104.0 }

 Darken a 0xRRGGBB toward dusk. The multiplier keeps the rock always below the
 snow.

  chan : Integer, Integer -> Real
  chan (color) (sh) = real-from-int (bit-and (bit-shr color sh) 255)

  dimmed : Integer, Real -> Integer
  dimmed (color) (dusk) =
    let f = 1.0 - rock-night-dim * dusk
    in let r = real-to-int (round-real (chan color 16 * f))
    in let g = real-to-int (round-real (chan color 8 * f))
    in let b = real-to-int (round-real (chan color 0 * f))
    in bit-or (bit-or (bit-shl r 16) (bit-shl g 8)) b

  snow-color : Real -> Integer
  snow-color (dusk) = pack (lerp3 snow-day snow-night dusk)

Section: Shape

  west-range-bearing : Real
  west-range-bearing = 0.0 - 2.0416

 Only ridge taller than this gets snow, and this is how far the snowline dips
 under the summit.

  snow-threshold : Real
  snow-threshold = 124.0

  snow-dip : Real
  snow-dip = 10.0

 Column sampling step, in pixels.

  col-step : Real
  col-step = 2.0

 The camera rolls with the rider's lean, up to about twenty degrees, so the
 rotated viewport sees design-x beyond the screen at the corners -- about
 seventy-four pixels at full lean. Trace this far past each edge so a lean never
 reveals sky past the backdrop's end.

  roll-margin : Real
  roll-margin = 200.0

 One range: a smooth envelope, tallest at centre and tapering to open sky at its
 edges, times a fixed rugged ridge line.

  range-at : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Real
  range-at (bearing) (center) (half) (peak) (freq-a) (freq-b) =
    let b = wrap (bearing - center) 64
    in let t = b / half
    in if real-abs t >= 1.0 then 0.0 else range-body b t peak freq-a freq-b

  range-body : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Real
  range-body (b) (t) (peak) (freq-a) (freq-b) =
    let envelope = r-cos (t * pi / 2.0)
    in let ridge = 0.6 + 0.24 * r-cos (b * freq-a) + 0.16 * r-cos (b * freq-b + 1.0)
    in peak * envelope * ridge

  ground-base : Real -> Real
  ground-base (bearing) = 18.0 + 12.0 * r-sin (wrap bearing 64 * 0.9 + 1.9)

  north-range : Real -> Real
  north-range (bearing) = range-at bearing 0.0 0.95 150.0 8.0 21.0

  west-range : Real -> Real
  west-range (bearing) = range-at bearing west-range-bearing 0.72 120.0 11.0 27.0

Section: The Snowline

 The north range's tallest hump, for normalising the snowline dip.

 THE ZIG ACCUMULATES ITS SAMPLE POSITION IN f32 AND THIS ACCUMULATES IN Real, so
 the two do not sample the same set: 0.01 is not exact in binary, and the f32 loop
 drifts far enough to run 101 times where this runs 100. Measured, that moves the
 peak by 8.5e-6 out of 147.54 -- 5.8e-8 relative -- because a maximum over a
 densely sampled smooth function does not care which of two adjacent samples it
 lands on. It is recorded because it is the only loop in this chapter whose STEP
 is inexact; the column loops step by 2.0 from -200.0, which is exact in both
 widths, and both sides walk 681 and 481 columns exactly.

  snow-peak-loop : Real, Real -> Real
  snow-peak-loop (b) (vm) =
    if b > 0.5 then vm
    else snow-peak-loop (b + 0.01) (real-max vm (north-range b))

  snow-peak-height : Real
  snow-peak-height = snow-peak-loop (0.0 - 0.5) (0.0 - 1.0)

 The zig calls the normalised fraction `above`, and that name cannot be used
 here: `above` is a Codex KEYWORD, one of the proof-language set beside `claim`,
 `proof` and `qed`. The refusal is correct and the diagnostic is not -- it comes
 out as CDX1023 "Expected 'in' after let bindings", naming the let rather than the
 keyword, which is a bisect to find. See PORTING_NOTES B11.

  snowline-at : Real, Real -> Real
  snowline-at (bearing) (peak) =
    let num = north-range bearing - snow-threshold
    in let above-frac = real-max 0.0 (real-min 1.0 (num / (peak - snow-threshold)))
    in snow-threshold - snow-dip * above-frac

Section: Columns and Crests

 A screen column maps to a bearing through the LIVE focal: a smaller cam-focal --
 lean, or a focus pull-in -- widens the angular span across the screen, exactly as
 the projection does. The centre follows the live width.

  bearing-at : Real, Real, Real, Real -> Real
  bearing-at (x) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) =
    heading + r-atan ((x - view-w / 2.0) / cam-focal)

 The silhouette crest at a bearing: the tallest of the ranges and the land.

  horizon-crest-px : Real -> Real
  horizon-crest-px (bearing) =
    real-max (west-range bearing) (real-max (north-range bearing) (ground-base bearing))

 Has the sun dropped fully BEHIND the western range? True once the whole disc is
 below the crest at the sun's bearing -- the moment the truck's headlights come on.

  sun-behind-mountains : Real -> Boolean
  sun-behind-mountains (step) =
    sun-height-px step + sun-radius-px < horizon-crest-px sun-bearing

Section: Silhouettes

 Trace a range's crest left to right, then close down to the horizon: one filled
 polygon. `v-scale` squeezes the heights vertically by the same factor the pull-in
 squeezes them horizontally, so it reads as a real focal change.

 The range is passed as a FUNCTION, the way the zig passes it as a comptime fn.

  crest-pts : (Real -> Real), Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List ScreenPt
  crest-pts (f) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (x) =
    if x > view-w + roll-margin then []
    else [ScreenPt { x = x, y = camera-h / 2.0 - f (bearing-at x heading cam-focal view-w) * v-scale }] & crest-pts f heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (x + col-step)

  silhouette : (Real -> Real), Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  silhouette (f) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (color) =
    let top = crest-pts f heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (0.0 - roll-margin)
    in let close = [ScreenPt { x = view-w + roll-margin, y = camera-h / 2.0 }, ScreenPt { x = 0.0 - roll-margin, y = camera-h / 2.0 }]
    in push-poly color (top & close)

Section: The Snowcap

 The band between the snowline and the crest, over the contiguous central hump
 where the north range rises above its snowline. Top edge left to right, bottom
 edge right to left. The TypeScript clips the range to the cap; this builds the
 band directly, which is the same shape without a clip primitive.

  snow-columns : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List Real
  snow-columns (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (peak) (x) =
    if x > view-w then []
    else snow-columns-at heading cam-focal view-w peak x

  snow-columns-at : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List Real
  snow-columns-at (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (peak) (x) =
    let b = bearing-at x heading cam-focal view-w
    in let rest = snow-columns heading cam-focal view-w peak (x + col-step)
    in if north-range b > snowline-at b peak + 0.01 then [x] & rest else rest

  snow-top : List Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  snow-top (xs) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then []
    else [ScreenPt { x = list-at xs i, y = camera-h / 2.0 - north-range (bearing-at (list-at xs i) heading cam-focal view-w) * v-scale }] & snow-top xs heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (i + 1)

  snow-bottom : List Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  snow-bottom (xs) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (peak) (i) =
    if i < 0 then []
    else [ScreenPt { x = list-at xs i, y = camera-h / 2.0 - snowline-at (bearing-at (list-at xs i) heading cam-focal view-w) peak * v-scale }] & snow-bottom xs heading cam-focal view-w v-scale peak (i - 1)

  draw-snow : Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  draw-snow (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (snow) =
    let peak = snow-peak-height
    in let xs = snow-columns heading cam-focal view-w peak 0.0
    in if list-length xs < 2 then []
    else push-poly snow ((snow-top xs heading cam-focal view-w v-scale 0) & snow-bottom xs heading cam-focal view-w v-scale peak (list-length xs - 1))

Section: The Frame

 Back to front: the westward range, the snowcapped northern range, and the rolling
 land. The rock and the snow dim toward dusk; the land does not.

  draw : Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw (heading) (dusk) (cam-focal) (view-w) =
    let v-scale = cam-focal / focal
    in let west = silhouette west-range heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (dimmed rock-west dusk)
    in let north = silhouette north-range heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (dimmed rock dusk)
    in let cap = draw-snow heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (snow-color dusk)
    in let ground = silhouette ground-base heading cam-focal view-w v-scale land
    in west & north & cap & ground

Chapter: Scene
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Tree
  cites Safari chapter Pond
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Safari chapter Mountains
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 A single frame of the Safari, computed in Codex.

 THROWAWAY, and deliberately so. This is the proof that Codex -> zig -> wasm ->
 the game's own JS blitter is a closed loop; it is not the faithful port. It
 places its own scenery instead of reading world.zig's route, and it carries its
 own Taylor sine instead of citing Gpu chapter DeviceMath, which the zig arm
 cannot transpile until real-from-int has an emitter. What it does share with the
 real thing is the part that matters: the camera, and the draw-command wire.

 The rider looks straight down a straight road, so there is no yaw rotation here
 -- geom.toRider collapses to a subtraction.

 The camera is NO LONGER hand-rolled. This chapter cites Safari chapter Camera,
 which is a faithful port of wasm/camera.zig graded against it at 1e-6 relative
 over 375 values, so the frame below is projected by the same lens the game uses
 -- FOCAL computed from the real field of view, not a number typed in here.

 We say:

Section: Draw Commands

 The wire of wasm/paint.zig: a tag, a colour, and a flat run of screen-space
 coordinates. tag 0 is a solid fill, which is all this frame uses.

  to-screen : List Vec3, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  to-screen (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [project (list-at ps i) focal camera-w] & to-screen ps (i + 1)

  solid : Integer, List Vec3 -> List DrawCmd
  solid (color) (ps) = push-poly color (to-screen ps 0)

Section: Ground

 The road is a flat quad on the ground plane, near edge first. A quad is exactly
 right for a plane: the projection is a perspective divide, so four projected
 corners bound the same region the surface does.

  road-half : Real
  road-half = 4.0

  road-near : Real
  road-near = 3.0

  road-far : Real
  road-far = 400.0

  road : List DrawCmd
  road =
    (solid 3815994
    [ Vec3 { right = 0.0 - road-half, forward = road-near, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = road-half, forward = road-near, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = road-half, forward = road-far, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.0 - road-half, forward = road-far, height = 0.0 } ])

  dash : Real -> List DrawCmd
  dash (a) =
    (solid 14211744
    [ Vec3 { right = 0.0 - 0.18, forward = a, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.18, forward = a, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.18, forward = a + 5.0, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.0 - 0.18, forward = a + 5.0, height = 0.0 } ])

  dashes : Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  dashes (a) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then []
    else (dash a) & dashes (a + 14.0) (fuel - 1)

Section: The Backdrop and the Clock

 THE RANGES ARE THE REAL ONES NOW. This section used to place two hand-made
 ridges as lists of heights at a fixed forward distance -- a screen-space cheat
 that looked like mountains and was not the game's. It is replaced by
 `Safari chapter Mountains`, a faithful port graded at 2,204 values, which
 samples a bearing per column through the live focal and traces the same
 silhouettes the zig does.

 The pose is chosen to put the most on screen at once. At this heading the WEST
 range fills the left, the snowcapped NORTH range enters from the right, and the
 sun is inside its visibility limit -- at heading 0 the sun is 2.2 radians off the
 look axis and the blitter would skip it entirely.

 ONE SCRUB KNOB, `u` in 0..1, drives the heading and the clock together. That is
 not a toy: the real game's advance() does exactly this -- the route turns the
 rider while the sunset clock runs -- and a single frame cannot eye-test either
 module. Panning is what shows a silhouette is a function of BEARING rather than a
 picture: the ranges slide past at their own rate, the snowcap comes round from
 the north as the sun leaves to the west, and the rocks dim as the sky reddens.

 The sweep runs from facing the sunset to facing north, because the two cannot be
 seen at once and the geometry says so: the sun sits at bearing -2.2176 and is
 skipped beyond 1.4 radians off the look axis, while the snowcap only clears its
 snowline near bearing 0. No heading holds both.

  scene-heading-at : Real -> Real
  scene-heading-at (u) = (0.0 - 2.5) + 3.1 * u

  scene-step-at : Real -> Real
  scene-step-at (u) = 3600.0 + 1400.0 * u

  scene-heading : Real
  scene-heading = scene-heading-at 0.0

 Late in the ride: the disc is a few pixels above the horizon, so the sunset
 warmth is near its peak and the sky is well into its dusk ramp.

  scene-step : Real
  scene-step = scene-step-at 0.0

  backdrop-at : Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  backdrop-at (heading) (step) = draw heading (sun-set-fraction step) focal camera-w

 The sky, the horizon band and the sun are NOT polygons. blitter.js paints them
 itself -- a linear gradient for the sky and a radial glow plus the disc for the
 sun -- and asks the module for the numbers. The shim exports these four straight
 out of the port, so what the browser paints behind the ranges is the ported
 sunset clock and nothing hand-tuned.

 THE SHIM CALLS `sky-color`, `horizon-color` AND `scene-sun-at` DIRECTLY, and
 there is a reason there are no per-field wrappers beside them. A Codex definition
 whose body is a single application is INLINED at its call sites and never becomes
 a zig function, so a `scene-sun-x-at (u) = (scene-sun-at u).x` written for the
 shim's convenience simply is not there to call -- six of them were, and the wasm
 build failed on six undeclared identifiers. The shim is outside the Codex
 program; it can only call what survives inlining. See PORTING_NOTES B12.

  scene-sun-at : Real -> SunPos
  scene-sun-at (u) = sun-pos (scene-heading-at u) (scene-step-at u) focal camera-w

Section: Trees

 Real conifers now: Safari chapter Tree, a faithful port of wasm/tree.zig graded
 against it over 45 draw commands and 628 coordinates. The two detail axes are set
 the way the renderer sets them -- a round-shaded trunk only on the nearest few,
 cone crowns within the detail distance and flat triangles beyond -- and the crown
 shade fades to zero with depth so there is no shaded-to-solid pop along the line.

  detail-dist : Real
  detail-dist = 60.0

  crown-shade : Real -> Real
  crown-shade (forward) =
    let s = 1.0 - forward / detail-dist
    in if s < 0.0 then 0.0 else s

  conifer : Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List DrawCmd
  conifer (right) (forward) (height) (round-trunk) =
    tree-draw right forward height 3108154 focal camera-w round-trunk (forward < detail-dist) (crown-shade forward)

Section: Pond

 The ported pond, placed off the left of the road. Its outline is Pond's own
 data, unchanged -- the chapter that Phase 0 graded against wasm/pond.zig, drawn
 here for the first time.

  pond-at-right : Real
  pond-at-right = 0.0 - 26.0

  pond-at-forward : Real
  pond-at-forward = 46.0

  pond-pts : List PondPt, Integer -> List Vec3
  pond-pts (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else let p = list-at ps i
      in [Vec3 { right = pond-at-right - p.cu, forward = pond-at-forward + p.cv, height = 0.0 }] & pond-pts ps (i + 1)

  pond-poly : List DrawCmd
  pond-poly = solid water-color (pond-pts water-outline 0)

  bank-poly : List DrawCmd
  bank-poly = solid bank-color (pond-pts bank 0)

Section: The Guard Rail

 The ported wasm/guard_rail.zig, on the right shoulder. Render builds this path
 along the outer edge of a corner; here the road is straight, so what is drawn is
 exactly the run-up section -- a post every four metres and a bar strip between
 them, receding to the horizon.

  rail-right : Real
  rail-right = road-half + 1.4

 The forward accumulator is a Real carried beside the counter, for the same reason
 the ridge's x is: no emitter for real-from-int, so a loop index cannot widen.

  rail-path : Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  rail-path (f) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then []
    else [RiderPt { right = rail-right, forward = f }] & rail-path (f - 4.0) (fuel - 1)

 SORTED BY THE DEPTH THE PORT COMPUTES. rail-emit hands back every bar and then
 every post, which is the order the zig's store holds them in and the wrong order
 to paint: a far post would land on top of a near bar. Each poly carries its own
 mean forward depth precisely so a caller can fix that, and this is the smallest
 possible demonstration of what that field is for -- the game merges rails, trees
 and critters into ONE such sort, which is why guard_rail collects instead of
 drawing.

  rail-insert : RailPoly, List RailPoly, Integer -> List RailPoly
  rail-insert (p) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then xs & [p]
    else if p.fwd > (list-at xs i).fwd then (list-take xs i) & [p] & (list-drop xs i)
    else rail-insert p xs (i + 1)

  rail-sort : List RailPoly, Integer, List RailPoly -> List RailPoly
  rail-sort (src) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length src then acc
    else rail-sort src (i + 1) (rail-insert (list-at src i) acc 0)

  rail-cmds : List DrawCmd
  rail-cmds = rail-draw-all (rail-sort (rail-emit (rail-path 96.0 21)) 0 []) focal camera-w 0

Section: The Frame

 Paint order, back to front: the ranges, then the ground furniture, then the
 nearer trees last so they occlude.

 The rail goes in as its own band, between the mid and near trees. That is a
 COARSE ordering where the game does one merged depth sort over all scenery, and
 it is right here only because the rail hugs the shoulder while every tree stands
 well outside it, so nothing overlaps. The rail's own polys are properly sorted;
 it is the merge with the trees that this throwaway does not do.

  frame-at : Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-at (u) =
    let ground = (backdrop-at (scene-heading-at u) (scene-step-at u)) & road & (dashes 12.0 24)
    in let water = bank-poly & pond-poly
    in let far-trees = (conifer (0.0 - 26.0) 165.0 11.0 False) & (conifer 21.0 132.0 9.5 False) & (conifer (0.0 - 17.0) 104.0 10.5 False) & (conifer 15.0 78.0 8.0 False)
    in let mid-trees = (conifer (0.0 - 13.0) 57.0 9.0 False) & (conifer 12.5 44.0 7.5 False) & (conifer (0.0 - 10.5) 33.0 8.5 False)
    in let near-trees = (conifer 9.5 23.0 7.0 True) & (conifer (0.0 - 8.0) 15.5 6.5 True) & (conifer 8.0 9.5 6.0 True)
    in ground & water & far-trees & mid-trees & rail-cmds & near-trees

  frame : List DrawCmd
  frame = frame-at 0.0

Chapter: SceneMain
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Poc chapter Scene

 The entry point. The wasm build never calls it -- the shim exports renderFrame
 and walks `frame` itself -- but the emitter only emits what the entry can reach,
 so opening must actually touch the scene or the whole chapter is shaken out.
 Reporting the command count keeps it reachable and doubles as a native smoke
 test: run this on the host and it says how many polygons the frame holds.

 THE SKY AND SUN LINES ARE NOT DECORATION. blitter.js paints those itself and
 asks the module for the numbers, so they never enter `frame` -- which means
 nothing reached them, which means the emitter shook all six out and the wasm
 build failed on six undeclared identifiers. Touching them here is what keeps
 them in the program. That is B6 in PORTING_NOTES, met for the second time in
 this same file.

 The sun's coordinates are printed through real-to-int, which is worth noticing:
 `show` on a Real is still refused, and before the conversion emitters landed
 this entry could not have reported them at all.

 We say:

Section: Entry

 One line per end of the sweep and its middle, which touches every export the
 shim calls and is a genuine smoke test of the clock: the sky must darken and the
 sun must set as u runs.

  report : Real -> Text
  report (u) =
    let sun = if (scene-sun-at u).visible then "sun on " else "sun off"
    in "sky " & show (sky-color (scene-step-at u)) & " horizon " & show (horizon-color (scene-step-at u)) & " " & sun & " x " & show (real-to-int (scene-sun-at u).x) & " y " & show (real-to-int (scene-sun-at u).y) & " cmds " & show (list-length (frame-at u))


  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("cmds  " & show (list-length frame))
    print-line-uni ("u=0.0 " & report 0.0)
    print-line-uni ("u=0.5 " & report 0.5)
    print-line-uni ("u=1.0 " & report 1.0)
  end
